.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/d3-scale Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "d3-scale"
.PP
Scales are a convenient abstraction for a fundamental task in visualization: mapping a dimension of abstract data to a visual representation\&. Although most often used for position-encoding quantitative data, such as mapping a measurement in meters to a position in pixels for dots in a scatterplot, scales can represent virtually any visual encoding, such as diverging colors, stroke widths, or symbol size\&. Scales can also be used with virtually any type of data, such as named categorical data or discrete data that requires sensible breaks\&.

.PP
For \fBcontinuous\fP quantitative data, you typically want a \fBlinear scale\fP\&. (For time series data, a \fBtime scale\fP\&.) If the distribution calls for it, consider transforming data using a \fBpower\fP or \fBlog\fP scale\&. A \fBquantize scale\fP may aid differentiation by rounding continuous data to a fixed set of discrete values; similarly, a \fBquantile scale\fP computes quantiles from a sample population, and a \fBthreshold scale\fP allows you to specify arbitrary breaks in continuous data\&.

.PP
For discrete ordinal (ordered) or categorical (unordered) data, an \fBordinal scale\fP specifies an explicit mapping from a set of data values to a corresponding set of visual attributes (such as colors)\&. The related \fBband\fP and \fBpoint\fP scales are useful for position-encoding ordinal data, such as bars in a bar chart or dots in an categorical scatterplot\&.

.PP
This repository does not provide color schemes; see \fRd3-scale-chromatic\fP for color schemes designed to work with d3-scale\&.

.PP
Scales have no intrinsic visual representation\&. However, most scales can \fBgenerate\fP and \fBformat\fP ticks for reference marks to aid in the construction of axes\&.

.PP
For a longer introduction, see these recommended tutorials:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRIntroducing d3-scale\fP by Mike Bostock
.IP "\(bu" 2
Chapter 7\&. Scales of \fR\fIInteractive Data Visualization for the Web\fP\fP by Scott Murray
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRd3: scales, and color\&.\fP by Jérôme Cukier
.PP
.SH "Installing"
.PP
If you use npm, \fRnpm install d3-scale\fP\&. You can also download the \fRlatest release on GitHub\fP\&. For vanilla HTML in modern browsers, import d3-scale from Skypack:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<script type="module">

import {scaleLinear} from "https://cdn\&.skypack\&.dev/d3\-scale@4";

const x = scaleLinear();

</script>
.fi
.PP

.PP
For legacy environments, you can load d3-scale’s UMD bundle from an npm-based CDN such as jsDelivr; a \fRd3\fP global is exported:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<script src="https://cdn\&.jsdelivr\&.net/npm/d3\-array@3"></script>
<script src="https://cdn\&.jsdelivr\&.net/npm/d3\-color@3"></script>
<script src="https://cdn\&.jsdelivr\&.net/npm/d3\-format@3"></script>
<script src="https://cdn\&.jsdelivr\&.net/npm/d3\-interpolate@3"></script>
<script src="https://cdn\&.jsdelivr\&.net/npm/d3\-time@3"></script>
<script src="https://cdn\&.jsdelivr\&.net/npm/d3\-time\-format@4"></script>
<script src="https://cdn\&.jsdelivr\&.net/npm/d3\-scale@4"></script>
<script>

const x = d3\&.scaleLinear();

</script>
.fi
.PP

.PP
(You can omit d3-time and d3-time-format if you’re not using \fBd3\&.scaleTime\fP or \fBd3\&.scaleUtc\fP\&.)
.SH "API Reference"
.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBContinuous\fP (\fBLinear\fP, \fBPower\fP, \fBLog\fP, \fBIdentity\fP, \fBTime\fP, \fBRadial\fP)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBSequential\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBDiverging\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBQuantize\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBQuantile\fP
