\fBOrdinal\fP (\fBBand\fP, \fBPoint\fP)
.PP
.SS "Continuous Scales"
Continuous scales map a continuous, quantitative input \fBdomain\fP to a continuous output \fBrange\fP\&. If the range is also numeric, the mapping may be \fBinverted\fP\&. A continuous scale is not constructed directly; instead, try a \fBlinear\fP, \fBpower\fP, \fBlog\fP, \fBidentity\fP, \fBradial\fP, \fBtime\fP or \fBsequential color\fP scale\&.

.PP
# \fIcontinuous\fP(\fIvalue\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Given a \fIvalue\fP from the \fBdomain\fP, returns the corresponding value from the \fBrange\fP\&. If the given \fIvalue\fP is outside the domain, and \fBclamping\fP is not enabled, the mapping may be extrapolated such that the returned value is outside the range\&. For example, to apply a position encoding:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var x = d3\&.scaleLinear()
    \&.domain([10, 130])
    \&.range([0, 960]);

x(20); // 80
x(50); // 320
.fi
.PP

.PP
Or to apply a color encoding:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var color = d3\&.scaleLinear()
    \&.domain([10, 100])
    \&.range(["brown", "steelblue"]);

color(20); // "#9a3439"
color(50); // "#7b5167"
.fi
.PP

.PP
Or, in shorthand:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var x = d3\&.scaleLinear([10, 130], [0, 960]);
var color = d3\&.scaleLinear([10, 100], ["brown", "steelblue"]);
.fi
.PP

.PP
# \fIcontinuous\fP\&.\fBinvert\fP(\fIvalue\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Given a \fIvalue\fP from the \fBrange\fP, returns the corresponding value from the \fBdomain\fP\&. Inversion is useful for interaction, say to determine the data value corresponding to the position of the mouse\&. For example, to invert a position encoding:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var x = d3\&.scaleLinear()
    \&.domain([10, 130])
    \&.range([0, 960]);

x\&.invert(80); // 20
x\&.invert(320); // 50
.fi
.PP

.PP
If the given \fIvalue\fP is outside the range, and \fBclamping\fP is not enabled, the mapping may be extrapolated such that the returned value is outside the domain\&. This method is only supported if the range is numeric\&. If the range is not numeric, returns NaN\&.

.PP
For a valid value \fIy\fP in the range, \fIcontinuous\fP(\fIcontinuous\fP\&.invert(\fIy\fP)) approximately equals \fIy\fP; similarly, for a valid value \fIx\fP in the domain, \fIcontinuous\fP\&.invert(\fIcontinuous\fP(\fIx\fP)) approximately equals \fIx\fP\&. The scale and its inverse may not be exact due to the limitations of floating point precision\&.

.PP
# \fIcontinuous\fP\&.\fBdomain\fP([\fIdomain\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIdomain\fP is specified, sets the scale’s domain to the specified array of numbers\&. The array must contain two or more elements\&. If the elements in the given array are not numbers, they will be coerced to numbers\&. If \fIdomain\fP is not specified, returns a copy of the scale’s current domain\&.

.PP
Although continuous scales typically have two values each in their domain and range, specifying more than two values produces a piecewise scale\&. For example, to create a \fBdiverging color scale\fP that interpolates between white and red for negative values, and white and green for positive values, say:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var color = d3\&.scaleLinear()
    \&.domain([\-1, 0, 1])
    \&.range(["red", "white", "green"]);

color(\-0\&.5); // "rgb(255, 128, 128)"
color(+0\&.5); // "rgb(128, 192, 128)"
.fi
.PP

.PP
Internally, a piecewise scale performs a \fRbinary search\fP for the range interpolator corresponding to the given domain value\&. Thus, the domain must be in ascending or descending order\&. If the domain and range have different lengths \fIN\fP and \fIM\fP, only the first \fImin(N,M)\fP elements in each are observed\&.

.PP
# \fIcontinuous\fP\&.\fBrange\fP([\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIrange\fP is specified, sets the scale’s range to the specified array of values\&. The array must contain two or more elements\&. Unlike the \fBdomain\fP, elements in the given array need not be numbers; any value that is supported by the underlying \fBinterpolator\fP will work, though note that numeric ranges are required for \fBinvert\fP\&. If \fIrange\fP is not specified, returns a copy of the scale’s current range\&. See \fB*continuous*\&.interpolate\fP for more examples\&.

.PP
# \fIcontinuous\fP\&.\fBrangeRound\fP([\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Sets the scale’s \fB*range*\fP to the specified array of values while also setting the scale’s \fBinterpolator\fP to \fRinterpolateRound\fP\&. This is a convenience method equivalent to:

.PP
.PP
.nf
continuous
    \&.range(range)
    \&.interpolate(d3\&.interpolateRound);
.fi
.PP

.PP
The rounding interpolator is sometimes useful for avoiding antialiasing artifacts, though also consider the \fRshape-rendering\fP “crispEdges” styles\&. Note that this interpolator can only be used with numeric ranges\&.

.PP
# \fIcontinuous\fP\&.\fBclamp\fP(\fIclamp\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIclamp\fP is specified, enables or disables clamping accordingly\&. If clamping is disabled and the scale is passed a value outside the \fBdomain\fP, the scale may return a value outside the \fBrange\fP through extrapolation\&. If clamping is enabled, the return value of the scale is always within the scale’s range\&. Clamping similarly applies to \fB*continuous*\&.invert\fP\&. For example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var x = d3\&.scaleLinear()
    \&.domain([10, 130])
    \&.range([0, 960]);

x(\-10); // \-160, outside range
x\&.invert(\-160); // \-10, outside domain

x\&.clamp(true);
x(\-10); // 0, clamped to range
x\&.invert(\-160); // 10, clamped to domain
.fi
.PP

.PP
If \fIclamp\fP is not specified, returns whether or not the scale currently clamps values to within the range\&.

.PP
# \fIcontinuous\fP\&.\fBunknown\fP([\fIvalue\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIvalue\fP is specified, sets the output value of the scale for undefined (or NaN) input values and returns this scale\&. If \fIvalue\fP is not specified, returns the current unknown value, which defaults to undefined\&.

.PP
# \fIcontinuous\fP\&.\fBinterpolate\fP(\fIinterpolate\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIinterpolate\fP is specified, sets the scale’s \fBrange\fP interpolator factory\&. This interpolator factory is used to create interpolators for each adjacent pair of values from the range; these interpolators then map a normalized domain parameter \fIt\fP in [0, 1] to the corresponding value in the range\&. If \fIfactory\fP is not specified, returns the scale’s current interpolator factory, which defaults to \fRd3\&.interpolate\fP\&. See \fRd3-interpolate\fP for more interpolators\&.

.PP
For example, consider a diverging color scale with three colors in the range:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var color = d3\&.scaleLinear()
    \&.domain([\-100, 0, +100])
    \&.range(["red", "white", "green"]);
.fi
.PP

.PP
Two interpolators are created internally by the scale, equivalent to:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var i0 = d3\&.interpolate("red", "white"),
    i1 = d3\&.interpolate("white", "green");
.fi
.PP

.PP
A common reason to specify a custom interpolator is to change the color space of interpolation\&. For example, to use \fRHCL\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var color = d3\&.scaleLinear()
    \&.domain([10, 100])
    \&.range(["brown", "steelblue"])
    \&.interpolate(d3\&.interpolateHcl);
.fi
.PP

.PP
Or for \fRCubehelix\fP with a custom gamma:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var color = d3\&.scaleLinear()
    \&.domain([10, 100])
    \&.range(["brown", "steelblue"])
    \&.interpolate(d3\&.interpolateCubehelix\&.gamma(3));
.fi
.PP

.PP
Note: the \fRdefault interpolator\fP \fBmay reuse return values\fP\&. For example, if the range values are objects, then the value interpolator always returns the same object, modifying it in-place\&. If the scale is used to set an attribute or style, this is typically acceptable (and desirable for performance); however, if you need to store the scale’s return value, you must specify your own interpolator or make a copy as appropriate\&.

.PP
# \fIcontinuous\fP\&.\fBticks\fP([\fIcount\fP])

.PP
Returns approximately \fIcount\fP representative values from the scale’s \fBdomain\fP\&. If \fIcount\fP is not specified, it defaults to 10\&. The returned tick values are uniformly spaced, have human-readable values (such as multiples of powers of 10), and are guaranteed to be within the extent of the domain\&. Ticks are often used to display reference lines, or tick marks, in conjunction with the visualized data\&. The specified \fIcount\fP is only a hint; the scale may return more or fewer values depending on the domain\&. See also d3-array’s \fRticks\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIcontinuous\fP\&.\fBtickFormat\fP([\fIcount\fP[, \fIspecifier\fP]]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Returns a \fRnumber format\fP function suitable for displaying a tick value, automatically computing the appropriate precision based on the fixed interval between tick values\&. The specified \fIcount\fP should have the same value as the count that is used to generate the \fBtick values\fP\&.

.PP
An optional \fIspecifier\fP allows a \fRcustom format\fP where the precision of the format is automatically set by the scale as appropriate for the tick interval\&. For example, to format percentage change, you might say:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var x = d3\&.scaleLinear()
    \&.domain([\-1, 1])
    \&.range([0, 960]);

var ticks = x\&.ticks(5),
    tickFormat = x\&.tickFormat(5, "+%");

ticks\&.map(tickFormat); // ["\-100%", "\-50%", "+0%", "+50%", "+100%"]
.fi
.PP

.PP
If \fIspecifier\fP uses the format type \fRs\fP, the scale will return a \fRSI-prefix format\fP based on the largest value in the domain\&. If the \fIspecifier\fP already specifies a precision, this method is equivalent to \fR\fIlocale\fP\&.format\fP\&.

.PP
See also \fBd3\&.tickFormat\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIcontinuous\fP\&.\fBnice\fP([\fIcount\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Extends the \fBdomain\fP so that it starts and ends on nice round values\&. This method typically modifies the scale’s domain, and may only extend the bounds to the nearest round value\&. An optional tick \fIcount\fP argument allows greater control over the step size used to extend the bounds, guaranteeing that the returned \fBticks\fP will exactly cover the domain\&. Nicing is useful if the domain is computed from data, say using \fRextent\fP, and may be irregular\&. For example, for a domain of [0\&.201479…, 0\&.996679…], a nice domain might be [0\&.2, 1\&.0]\&. If the domain has more than two values, nicing the domain only affects the first and last value\&. See also d3-array’s \fRtickStep\fP\&.

.PP
Nicing a scale only modifies the current domain; it does not automatically nice domains that are subsequently set using \fB*continuous*\&.domain\fP\&. You must re-nice the scale after setting the new domain, if desired\&.

.PP
# \fIcontinuous\fP\&.\fBcopy\fP() · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Returns an exact copy of this scale\&. Changes to this scale will not affect the returned scale, and vice versa\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fBtickFormat\fP(\fIstart\fP, \fIstop\fP, \fIcount\fP[, \fIspecifier\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Returns a \fRnumber format\fP function suitable for displaying a tick value, automatically computing the appropriate precision based on the fixed interval between tick values, as determined by \fRd3\&.tickStep\fP\&.

.PP
An optional \fIspecifier\fP allows a \fRcustom format\fP where the precision of the format is automatically set by the scale as appropriate for the tick interval\&. For example, to format percentage change, you might say:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var tickFormat = d3\&.tickFormat(\-1, 1, 5, "+%");

tickFormat(\-0\&.5); // "\-50%"
.fi
.PP

.PP
If \fIspecifier\fP uses the format type \fRs\fP, the scale will return a \fRSI-prefix format\fP based on the larger absolute value of \fIstart\fP and \fIstop\fP\&. If the \fIspecifier\fP already specifies a precision, this method is equivalent to \fR\fIlocale\fP\&.format\fP\&.
.SS "Linear Scales"
# d3\&.\fBscaleLinear\fP([[\fIdomain\fP, ]\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Constructs a new \fBcontinuous scale\fP with the specified \fBdomain\fP and \fBrange\fP, the \fRdefault\fP \fBinterpolator\fP and \fBclamping\fP disabled\&. If either \fIdomain\fP or \fIrange\fP are not specified, each defaults to [0, 1]\&. Linear scales are a good default choice for continuous quantitative data because they preserve proportional differences\&. Each range value \fIy\fP can be expressed as a function of the domain value \fIx\fP: \fIy\fP = \fImx\fP + \fIb\fP\&.
.SS "Power Scales"
Power scales are similar to \fBlinear scales\fP, except an exponential transform is applied to the input domain value before the output range value is computed\&. Each range value \fIy\fP can be expressed as a function of the domain value \fIx\fP: \fIy\fP = \fImx^k\fP + \fIb\fP, where \fIk\fP is the \fBexponent\fP value\&. Power scales also support negative domain values, in which case the input value and the resulting output value are multiplied by -1\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fBscalePow\fP([[\fIdomain\fP, ]\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Constructs a new \fBcontinuous scale\fP with the specified \fBdomain\fP and \fBrange\fP, the \fBexponent\fP 1, the \fRdefault\fP \fBinterpolator\fP and \fBclamping\fP disabled\&. If either \fIdomain\fP or \fIrange\fP are not specified, each defaults to [0, 1]\&. (Note that this is effectively a \fBlinear\fP scale until you set a different exponent\&.)

.PP
# \fIpow\fP(\fIvalue\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIpow\fP\&.\fBinvert\fP(\fIvalue\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.invert\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIpow\fP\&.\fBexponent\fP([\fIexponent\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIexponent\fP is specified, sets the current exponent to the given numeric value\&. If \fIexponent\fP is not specified, returns the current exponent, which defaults to 1\&. (Note that this is effectively a \fBlinear\fP scale until you set a different exponent\&.)

.PP
# \fIpow\fP\&.\fBdomain\fP([\fIdomain\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.domain\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIpow\fP\&.\fBrange\fP([\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.range\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIpow\fP\&.\fBrangeRound\fP([\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.rangeRound\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIpow\fP\&.\fBclamp\fP(\fIclamp\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.clamp\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIpow\fP\&.\fBinterpolate\fP(\fIinterpolate\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.interpolate\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIpow\fP\&.\fBticks\fP([\fIcount\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.ticks\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIpow\fP\&.\fBtickFormat\fP([\fIcount\fP[, \fIspecifier\fP]]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.tickFormat\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIpow\fP\&.\fBnice\fP([\fIcount\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.nice\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIpow\fP\&.\fBcopy\fP() · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.copy\fP\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fBscaleSqrt\fP([[\fIdomain\fP, ]\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Constructs a new \fBcontinuous\fP \fBpower scale\fP with the specified \fBdomain\fP and \fBrange\fP, the \fBexponent\fP 0\&.5, the \fRdefault\fP \fBinterpolator\fP and \fBclamping\fP disabled\&. If either \fIdomain\fP or \fIrange\fP are not specified, each defaults to [0, 1]\&. This is a convenience method equivalent to \fRd3\&.scalePow(…)\&.exponent(0\&.5)\fP\&.
.SS "Log Scales"
Log scales are similar to \fBlinear scales\fP, except a logarithmic transform is applied to the input domain value before the output range value is computed\&. The mapping to the range value \fIy\fP can be expressed as a function of the domain value \fIx\fP: \fIy\fP = \fIm\fP log(\fIx\fP) + \fIb\fP\&.

.PP
As log(0) = -∞, a log scale domain must be \fBstrictly-positive or strictly-negative\fP; the domain must not include or cross zero\&. A log scale with a positive domain has a well-defined behavior for positive values, and a log scale with a negative domain has a well-defined behavior for negative values\&. (For a negative domain, input and output values are implicitly multiplied by -1\&.) The behavior of the scale is undefined if you pass a negative value to a log scale with a positive domain or vice versa\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fBscaleLog\fP([[\fIdomain\fP, ]\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Constructs a new \fBcontinuous scale\fP with the specified \fBdomain\fP and \fBrange\fP, the \fBbase\fP 10, the \fRdefault\fP \fBinterpolator\fP and \fBclamping\fP disabled\&. If \fIdomain\fP is not specified, it defaults to [1, 10]\&. If \fIrange\fP is not specified, it defaults to [0, 1]\&.

.PP
# \fIlog\fP(\fIvalue\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIlog\fP\&.\fBinvert\fP(\fIvalue\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.invert\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIlog\fP\&.\fBbase\fP([\fIbase\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIbase\fP is specified, sets the base for this logarithmic scale to the specified value\&. If \fIbase\fP is not specified, returns the current base, which defaults to 10\&.

.PP
# \fIlog\fP\&.\fBdomain\fP([\fIdomain\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.domain\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIlog\fP\&.\fBrange\fP([\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.range\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIlog\fP\&.\fBrangeRound\fP([\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.rangeRound\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIlog\fP\&.\fBclamp\fP(\fIclamp\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.clamp\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIlog\fP\&.\fBinterpolate\fP(\fIinterpolate\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.interpolate\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIlog\fP\&.\fBticks\fP([\fIcount\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Like \fB*continuous*\&.ticks\fP, but customized for a log scale\&. If the \fBbase\fP is an integer, the returned ticks are uniformly spaced within each integer power of base; otherwise, one tick per power of base is returned\&. The returned ticks are guaranteed to be within the extent of the domain\&. If the orders of magnitude in the \fBdomain\fP is greater than \fIcount\fP, then at most one tick per power is returned\&. Otherwise, the tick values are unfiltered, but note that you can use \fB*log*\&.tickFormat\fP to filter the display of tick labels\&. If \fIcount\fP is not specified, it defaults to 10\&.

.PP
# \fIlog\fP\&.\fBtickFormat\fP([\fIcount\fP[, \fIspecifier\fP]]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Like \fB*continuous*\&.tickFormat\fP, but customized for a log scale\&. The specified \fIcount\fP typically has the same value as the count that is used to generate the \fBtick values\fP\&. If there are too many ticks, the formatter may return the empty string for some of the tick labels; however, note that the ticks are still shown\&. To disable filtering, specify a \fIcount\fP of Infinity\&. When specifying a count, you may also provide a format \fIspecifier\fP or format function\&. For example, to get a tick formatter that will display 20 ticks of a currency, say \fRlog\&.tickFormat(20, "$,f")\fP\&. If the specifier does not have a defined precision, the precision will be set automatically by the scale, returning the appropriate format\&. This provides a convenient way of specifying a format whose precision will be automatically set by the scale\&.

.PP
# \fIlog\fP\&.\fBnice\fP() · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Like \fB*continuous*\&.nice\fP, except extends the domain to integer powers of \fBbase\fP\&. For example, for a domain of [0\&.201479…, 0\&.996679…], and base 10, the nice domain is [0\&.1, 1]\&. If the domain has more than two values, nicing the domain only affects the first and last value\&.

.PP
# \fIlog\fP\&.\fBcopy\fP() · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.copy\fP\&.
.SS "Symlog Scales"
See \fRA bi-symmetric log transformation for wide-range data\fP by Webber for more\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fBscaleSymlog\fP([[\fIdomain\fP, ]\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Constructs a new \fBcontinuous scale\fP with the specified \fBdomain\fP and \fBrange\fP, the \fBconstant\fP 1, the \fRdefault\fP \fBinterpolator\fP and \fBclamping\fP disabled\&. If \fIdomain\fP is not specified, it defaults to [0, 1]\&. If \fIrange\fP is not specified, it defaults to [0, 1]\&.

.PP
# \fIsymlog\fP\&.\fBconstant\fP([\fIconstant\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIconstant\fP is specified, sets the symlog constant to the specified number and returns this scale; otherwise returns the current value of the symlog constant, which defaults to 1\&. See “A bi-symmetric log transformation for wide-range data” by Webber for more\&.
.SS "Identity Scales"
Identity scales are a special case of \fBlinear scales\fP where the domain and range are identical; the scale and its invert method are thus the identity function\&. These scales are occasionally useful when working with pixel coordinates, say in conjunction with an axis\&. Identity scales do not support \fBrangeRound\fP, \fBclamp\fP or \fBinterpolate\fP\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fBscaleIdentity\fP([\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Constructs a new identity scale with the specified \fBdomain\fP and \fBrange\fP\&. If \fIrange\fP is not specified, it defaults to [0, 1]\&.
.SS "Radial Scales"
Radial scales are a variant of \fBlinear scales\fP where the range is internally squared so that an input value corresponds linearly to the squared output value\&. These scales are useful when you want the input value to correspond to the area of a graphical mark and the mark is specified by radius, as in a radial bar chart\&. Radial scales do not support \fBinterpolate\fP\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fBscaleRadial\fP([[\fIdomain\fP, ]\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Constructs a new radial scale with the specified \fBdomain\fP and \fBrange\fP\&. If \fIdomain\fP or \fIrange\fP is not specified, each defaults to [0, 1]\&.
.SS "Time Scales"
Time scales are a variant of \fBlinear scales\fP that have a temporal domain: domain values are coerced to \fRdates\fP rather than numbers, and \fBinvert\fP likewise returns a date\&. Time scales implement \fBticks\fP based on \fRcalendar intervals\fP, taking the pain out of generating axes for temporal domains\&.

.PP
For example, to create a position encoding:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var x = d3\&.scaleTime()
    \&.domain([new Date(2000, 0, 1), new Date(2000, 0, 2)])
    \&.range([0, 960]);

x(new Date(2000, 0, 1,  5)); // 200
x(new Date(2000, 0, 1, 16)); // 640
x\&.invert(200); // Sat Jan 01 2000 05:00:00 GMT\-0800 (PST)
x\&.invert(640); // Sat Jan 01 2000 16:00:00 GMT\-0800 (PST)
.fi
.PP

.PP
For a valid value \fIy\fP in the range, \fItime\fP(\fItime\fP\&.invert(\fIy\fP)) equals \fIy\fP; similarly, for a valid value \fIx\fP in the domain, \fItime\fP\&.invert(\fItime\fP(\fIx\fP)) equals \fIx\fP\&. The invert method is useful for interaction, say to determine the value in the domain that corresponds to the pixel location under the mouse\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fBscaleTime\fP([[\fIdomain\fP, ]\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Constructs a new time scale with the specified \fBdomain\fP and \fBrange\fP, the \fRdefault\fP \fBinterpolator\fP and \fBclamping\fP disabled\&. If \fIdomain\fP is not specified, it defaults to [2000-01-01, 2000-01-02]\&. If \fIrange\fP is not specified, it defaults to [0, 1]\&.

.PP
# \fItime\fP(\fIvalue\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\fP\&.

.PP
# \fItime\fP\&.\fBinvert\fP(\fIvalue\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.invert\fP\&.

.PP
# \fItime\fP\&.\fBdomain\fP([\fIdomain\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.domain\fP\&.

.PP
# \fItime\fP\&.\fBrange\fP([\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.range\fP\&.

.PP
# \fItime\fP\&.\fBrangeRound\fP([\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.rangeRound\fP\&.

.PP
# \fItime\fP\&.\fBclamp\fP(\fIclamp\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.clamp\fP\&.

.PP
# \fItime\fP\&.\fBinterpolate\fP(\fIinterpolate\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.interpolate\fP\&.

.PP
# \fItime\fP\&.\fBticks\fP([\fIcount\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP 
.br
# \fItime\fP\&.\fBticks\fP([\fIinterval\fP])

.PP
Returns representative dates from the scale’s \fBdomain\fP\&. The returned tick values are uniformly-spaced (mostly), have sensible values (such as every day at midnight), and are guaranteed to be within the extent of the domain\&. Ticks are often used to display reference lines, or tick marks, in conjunction with the visualized data\&.

.PP
An optional \fIcount\fP may be specified to affect how many ticks are generated\&. If \fIcount\fP is not specified, it defaults to 10\&. The specified \fIcount\fP is only a hint; the scale may return more or fewer values depending on the domain\&. For example, to create ten default ticks, say:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var x = d3\&.scaleTime();

x\&.ticks(10);
// [Sat Jan 01 2000 00:00:00 GMT\-0800 (PST),
//  Sat Jan 01 2000 03:00:00 GMT\-0800 (PST),
//  Sat Jan 01 2000 06:00:00 GMT\-0800 (PST),
//  Sat Jan 01 2000 09:00:00 GMT\-0800 (PST),
//  Sat Jan 01 2000 12:00:00 GMT\-0800 (PST),
//  Sat Jan 01 2000 15:00:00 GMT\-0800 (PST),
//  Sat Jan 01 2000 18:00:00 GMT\-0800 (PST),
//  Sat Jan 01 2000 21:00:00 GMT\-0800 (PST),
//  Sun Jan 02 2000 00:00:00 GMT\-0800 (PST)]
.fi
.PP

.PP
The following time intervals are considered for automatic ticks:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
1-, 5-, 15- and 30-second\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
1-, 5-, 15- and 30-minute\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
1-, 3-, 6- and 12-hour\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
1- and 2-day\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
1-week\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
1- and 3-month\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
1-year\&.
.PP

.PP
In lieu of a \fIcount\fP, a \fRtime \fIinterval\fP\fP may be explicitly specified\&. To prune the generated ticks for a given time \fIinterval\fP, use \fR\fIinterval\fP\&.every\fP\&. For example, to generate ticks at 15-\fRminute\fP intervals:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var x = d3\&.scaleTime()
    \&.domain([new Date(2000, 0, 1, 0), new Date(2000, 0, 1, 2)]);

x\&.ticks(d3\&.timeMinute\&.every(15));
// [Sat Jan 01 2000 00:00:00 GMT\-0800 (PST),
//  Sat Jan 01 2000 00:15:00 GMT\-0800 (PST),
//  Sat Jan 01 2000 00:30:00 GMT\-0800 (PST),
//  Sat Jan 01 2000 00:45:00 GMT\-0800 (PST),
//  Sat Jan 01 2000 01:00:00 GMT\-0800 (PST),
//  Sat Jan 01 2000 01:15:00 GMT\-0800 (PST),
//  Sat Jan 01 2000 01:30:00 GMT\-0800 (PST),
//  Sat Jan 01 2000 01:45:00 GMT\-0800 (PST),
//  Sat Jan 01 2000 02:00:00 GMT\-0800 (PST)]
.fi
.PP

.PP
Alternatively, pass a test function to \fR\fIinterval\fP\&.filter\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
x\&.ticks(d3\&.timeMinute\&.filter(function(d) {
  return d\&.getMinutes() % 15 === 0;
}));
.fi
.PP

.PP
Note: in some cases, such as with day ticks, specifying a \fIstep\fP can result in irregular spacing of ticks because time intervals have varying length\&.

.PP
# \fItime\fP\&.\fBtickFormat\fP([\fIcount\fP[, \fIspecifier\fP]]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP 
.br
\fR#\fP \fItime\fP\&.\fBtickFormat\fP([\fIinterval\fP[, \fIspecifier\fP]])

.PP
Returns a time format function suitable for displaying \fBtick\fP values\&. The specified \fIcount\fP or \fIinterval\fP is currently ignored, but is accepted for consistency with other scales such as \fB*continuous*\&.tickFormat\fP\&. If a format \fIspecifier\fP is specified, this method is equivalent to \fRformat\fP\&. If \fIspecifier\fP is not specified, the default time format is returned\&. The default multi-scale time format chooses a human-readable representation based on the specified date as follows:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRY\fP - for year boundaries, such as \fR2011\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRB\fP - for month boundaries, such as \fRFebruary\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRb d\fP - for week boundaries, such as \fRFeb 06\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRa d\fP - for day boundaries, such as \fRMon 07\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRI p\fP - for hour boundaries, such as \fR01 AM\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRI:M\fP - for minute boundaries, such as \fR01:23\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR:S\fP - for second boundaries, such as \fR:45\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\&.L\fP - milliseconds for all other times, such as \fR\&.012\fP\&.
.PP

.PP
Although somewhat unusual, this default behavior has the benefit of providing both local and global context: for example, formatting a sequence of ticks as [11 PM, Mon 07, 01 AM] reveals information about hours, dates, and day simultaneously, rather than just the hours [11 PM, 12 AM, 01 AM]\&. See \fRd3-time-format\fP if you’d like to roll your own conditional time format\&.

.PP
# \fItime\fP\&.\fBnice\fP([\fIcount\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP 
.br
# \fItime\fP\&.\fBnice\fP([\fIinterval\fP])

.PP
Extends the \fBdomain\fP so that it starts and ends on nice round values\&. This method typically modifies the scale’s domain, and may only extend the bounds to the nearest round value\&. See \fB*continuous*\&.nice\fP for more\&.

.PP
An optional tick \fIcount\fP argument allows greater control over the step size used to extend the bounds, guaranteeing that the returned \fBticks\fP will exactly cover the domain\&. Alternatively, a \fRtime \fIinterval\fP\fP may be specified to explicitly set the ticks\&. If an \fIinterval\fP is specified, an optional \fIstep\fP may also be specified to skip some ticks\&. For example, \fRtime\&.nice(d3\&.timeSecond\&.every(10))\fP will extend the domain to an even ten seconds (0, 10, 20, \fIetc\&.\fP)\&. See \fB*time*\&.ticks\fP and \fR\fIinterval\fP\&.every\fP for further detail\&.

.PP
Nicing is useful if the domain is computed from data, say using \fRextent\fP, and may be irregular\&. For example, for a domain of [2009-07-13T00:02, 2009-07-13T23:48], the nice domain is [2009-07-13, 2009-07-14]\&. If the domain has more than two values, nicing the domain only affects the first and last value\&.

.PP
# \fItime\fP\&.\fBcopy\fP() · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.copy\fP\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fBscaleUtc\fP([[\fIdomain\fP, ]\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Equivalent to \fBscaleTime\fP, but the returned time scale operates in \fRCoordinated Universal Time\fP rather than local time\&.
.SS "Sequential Scales"
Sequential scales, like \fBdiverging scales\fP, are similar to \fBcontinuous scales\fP in that they map a continuous, numeric input domain to a continuous output range\&. However, unlike continuous scales, the input domain and output range of a sequential scale always has exactly two elements, and the output range is typically specified as an interpolator rather than an array of values\&. These scales do not expose \fBinvert\fP and \fBinterpolate\fP methods\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fBscaleSequential\fP([[\fIdomain\fP, ]\fIinterpolator\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Constructs a new sequential scale with the specified \fB*domain*\fP and \fB*interpolator*\fP function or array\&. If \fIdomain\fP is not specified, it defaults to [0, 1]\&. If \fIinterpolator\fP is not specified, it defaults to the identity function\&. When the scale is \fBapplied\fP, the interpolator will be invoked with a value typically in the range [0, 1], where 0 represents the minimum value and 1 represents the maximum value\&. For example, to implement the ill-advised \fRHSL\fP rainbow scale:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var rainbow = d3\&.scaleSequential(function(t) {
  return d3\&.hsl(t * 360, 1, 0\&.5) + "";
});
.fi
.PP

.PP
A more aesthetically-pleasing and perceptually-effective cyclical hue encoding is to use \fRd3\&.interpolateRainbow\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var rainbow = d3\&.scaleSequential(d3\&.interpolateRainbow);
.fi
.PP

.PP
If \fIinterpolator\fP is an array, it represents the scale’s two-element output range and is converted to an interpolator function using \fRd3\&.interpolate\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIsequential\fP(\fIvalue\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIsequential\fP\&.\fBdomain\fP([\fIdomain\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.domain\fP\&. Note that a sequential scale’s domain must be numeric and must contain exactly two values\&.

.PP
# \fIsequential\fP\&.\fBclamp\fP([\fIclamp\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.clamp\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIsequential\fP\&.\fBinterpolator\fP([\fIinterpolator\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIinterpolator\fP is specified, sets the scale’s interpolator to the specified function\&. If \fIinterpolator\fP is not specified, returns the scale’s current interpolator\&.

.PP
# \fIsequential\fP\&.\fBrange\fP([\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.range\fP\&. If \fIrange\fP is specified, the given two-element array is converted to an interpolator function using \fRd3\&.interpolate\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIsequential\fP\&.\fBrangeRound\fP([\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.rangeRound\fP\&. If \fIrange\fP is specified, implicitly uses \fRd3\&.interpolateRound\fP as the interpolator\&.

.PP
# \fIsequential\fP\&.\fBcopy\fP() · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.copy\fP\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fBscaleSequentialLog\fP([[\fIdomain\fP, ]\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
A \fBsequential scale\fP with a logarithmic transform, analogous to a \fBlog scale\fP\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fBscaleSequentialPow\fP([[\fIdomain\fP, ]\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
A \fBsequential scale\fP with an exponential transform, analogous to a \fBpower scale\fP\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fBscaleSequentialSqrt\fP([[\fIdomain\fP, ]\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
A \fBsequential scale\fP with a square-root transform, analogous to a \fBd3\&.scaleSqrt\fP\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fBscaleSequentialSymlog\fP([[\fIdomain\fP, ]\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
A \fBsequential scale\fP with a symmetric logarithmic transform, analogous to a \fBsymlog scale\fP\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fBscaleSequentialQuantile\fP([[\fIdomain\fP, ]\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
A \fBsequential scale\fP using a \fIp\fP-quantile transform, analogous to a \fBquantile scale\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIsequentialQuantile\fP\&.\fBquantiles\fP(\fIn\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Returns an array of \fIn\fP + 1 quantiles\&. For example, if \fIn\fP = 4, returns an array of five numbers: the minimum value, the first quartile, the median, the third quartile, and the maximum\&.
.SS "Diverging Scales"
Diverging scales, like \fBsequential scales\fP, are similar to \fBcontinuous scales\fP in that they map a continuous, numeric input domain to a continuous output range\&. However, unlike continuous scales, the input domain and output range of a diverging scale always has exactly three elements, and the output range is typically specified as an interpolator rather than an array of values\&. These scales do not expose \fBinvert\fP and \fBinterpolate\fP methods\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fBscaleDiverging\fP([[\fIdomain\fP, ]\fIinterpolator\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Constructs a new diverging scale with the specified \fB*domain*\fP and \fB*interpolator*\fP function or array\&. If \fIdomain\fP is not specified, it defaults to [0, 0\&.5, 1]\&. If \fIinterpolator\fP is not specified, it defaults to the identity function\&. When the scale is \fBapplied\fP, the interpolator will be invoked with a value typically in the range [0, 1], where 0 represents the extreme negative value, 0\&.5 represents the neutral value, and 1 represents the extreme positive value\&. For example, using \fRd3\&.interpolateSpectral\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var spectral = d3\&.scaleDiverging(d3\&.interpolateSpectral);
.fi
.PP

.PP
If \fIinterpolator\fP is an array, it represents the scale’s three-element output range and is converted to an interpolator function using \fRd3\&.interpolate\fP and \fRd3\&.piecewise\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIdiverging\fP(\fIvalue\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIdiverging\fP\&.\fBdomain\fP([\fIdomain\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.domain\fP\&. Note that a diverging scale’s domain must be numeric and must contain exactly three values\&. The default domain is [0, 0\&.5, 1]\&.

.PP
# \fIdiverging\fP\&.\fBclamp\fP([\fIclamp\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.clamp\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIdiverging\fP\&.\fBinterpolator\fP([\fIinterpolator\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIinterpolator\fP is specified, sets the scale’s interpolator to the specified function\&. If \fIinterpolator\fP is not specified, returns the scale’s current interpolator\&.

.PP
# \fIdiverging\fP\&.\fBrange\fP([\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.range\fP\&. If \fIrange\fP is specified, the given three-element array is converted to an interpolator function using \fRd3\&.interpolate\fP and \fRd3\&.piecewise\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIdiverging\fP\&.\fBrangeRound\fP([\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.range\fP\&. If \fIrange\fP is specified, implicitly uses \fRd3\&.interpolateRound\fP as the interpolator\&.

.PP
# \fIdiverging\fP\&.\fBcopy\fP() · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.copy\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIdiverging\fP\&.\fBunknown\fP() · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
See \fB*continuous*\&.unknown\fP\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fBscaleDivergingLog\fP([[\fIdomain\fP, ]\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
A \fBdiverging scale\fP with a logarithmic transform, analogous to a \fBlog scale\fP\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fBscaleDivergingPow\fP([[\fIdomain\fP, ]\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
A \fBdiverging scale\fP with an exponential transform, analogous to a \fBpower scale\fP\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fBscaleDivergingSqrt\fP([[\fIdomain\fP, ]\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
A \fBdiverging scale\fP with a square-root transform, analogous to a \fBd3\&.scaleSqrt\fP\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fBscaleDivergingSymlog\fP([[\fIdomain\fP, ]\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
A \fBdiverging scale\fP with a symmetric logarithmic transform, analogous to a \fBsymlog scale\fP\&.
.SS "Quantize Scales"
Quantize scales are similar to \fBlinear scales\fP, except they use a discrete rather than continuous range\&. The continuous input domain is divided into uniform segments based on the number of values in (\fIi\&.e\&.\fP, the cardinality of) the output range\&. Each range value \fIy\fP can be expressed as a quantized linear function of the domain value \fIx\fP: \fIy\fP = \fIm round(x)\fP + \fIb\fP\&. See \fRthis choropleth\fP for an example\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fBscaleQuantize\fP([[\fIdomain\fP, ]\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Constructs a new quantize scale with the specified \fB*domain*\fP and \fB*range*\fP\&. If either \fIdomain\fP or \fIrange\fP is not specified, each defaults to [0, 1]\&. Thus, the default quantize scale is equivalent to the \fRMath\&.round\fP function\&.

.PP
# \fIquantize\fP(\fIvalue\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Given a \fIvalue\fP in the input \fBdomain\fP, returns the corresponding value in the output \fBrange\fP\&. For example, to apply a color encoding:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var color = d3\&.scaleQuantize()
    \&.domain([0, 1])
    \&.range(["brown", "steelblue"]);

color(0\&.49); // "brown"
color(0\&.51); // "steelblue"
.fi
.PP

.PP
Or dividing the domain into three equally-sized parts with different range values to compute an appropriate stroke width:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var width = d3\&.scaleQuantize()
    \&.domain([10, 100])
    \&.range([1, 2, 4]);

width(20); // 1
width(50); // 2
width(80); // 4
.fi
.PP

.PP
# \fIquantize\fP\&.\fBinvertExtent\fP(\fIvalue\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Returns the extent of values in the \fBdomain\fP [\fIx0\fP, \fIx1\fP] for the corresponding \fIvalue\fP in the \fBrange\fP: the inverse of \fB*quantize*\fP\&. This method is useful for interaction, say to determine the value in the domain that corresponds to the pixel location under the mouse\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
var width = d3\&.scaleQuantize()
    \&.domain([10, 100])
    \&.range([1, 2, 4]);

width\&.invertExtent(2); // [40, 70]
.fi
.PP

.PP
# \fIquantize\fP\&.\fBdomain\fP([\fIdomain\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIdomain\fP is specified, sets the scale’s domain to the specified two-element array of numbers\&. If the elements in the given array are not numbers, they will be coerced to numbers\&. The numbers must be in ascending order or the behavior of the scale is undefined\&. If \fIdomain\fP is not specified, returns the scale’s current domain\&.

.PP
# \fIquantize\fP\&.\fBrange\fP([\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIrange\fP is specified, sets the scale’s range to the specified array of values\&. The array may contain any number of discrete values\&. The elements in the given array need not be numbers; any value or type will work\&. If \fIrange\fP is not specified, returns the scale’s current range\&.

.PP
# \fIquantize\fP\&.\fBticks\fP([\fIcount\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Equivalent to \fB*continuous*\&.ticks\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIquantize\fP\&.\fBtickFormat\fP([\fIcount\fP[, \fIspecifier\fP]]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Equivalent to \fB*continuous*\&.tickFormat\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIquantize\fP\&.\fBnice\fP() · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Equivalent to \fB*continuous*\&.nice\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIquantize\fP\&.\fBthresholds\fP() · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Returns the array of computed thresholds within the \fBdomain\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIquantize\fP\&.\fBcopy\fP() · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Returns an exact copy of this scale\&. Changes to this scale will not affect the returned scale, and vice versa\&.
.SS "Quantile Scales"
Quantile scales map a sampled input domain to a discrete range\&. The domain is considered continuous and thus the scale will accept any reasonable input value; however, the domain is specified as a discrete set of sample values\&. The number of values in (the cardinality of) the output range determines the number of quantiles that will be computed from the domain\&. To compute the quantiles, the domain is sorted, and treated as a \fRpopulation of discrete values\fP; see d3-array’s \fRquantile\fP\&. See \fRthis quantile choropleth\fP for an example\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fBscaleQuantile\fP([[\fIdomain\fP, ]\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Constructs a new quantile scale with the specified \fB*domain*\fP and \fB*range*\fP\&. If either \fIdomain\fP or \fIrange\fP is not specified, each defaults to the empty array\&. The quantile scale is invalid until both a domain and range are specified\&.

.PP
# \fIquantile\fP(\fIvalue\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Given a \fIvalue\fP in the input \fBdomain\fP, returns the corresponding value in the output \fBrange\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIquantile\fP\&.\fBinvertExtent\fP(\fIvalue\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Returns the extent of values in the \fBdomain\fP [\fIx0\fP, \fIx1\fP] for the corresponding \fIvalue\fP in the \fBrange\fP: the inverse of \fB*quantile*\fP\&. This method is useful for interaction, say to determine the value in the domain that corresponds to the pixel location under the mouse\&.

.PP
# \fIquantile\fP\&.\fBdomain\fP([\fIdomain\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIdomain\fP is specified, sets the domain of the quantile scale to the specified set of discrete numeric values\&. The array must not be empty, and must contain at least one numeric value; NaN, null and undefined values are ignored and not considered part of the sample population\&. If the elements in the given array are not numbers, they will be coerced to numbers\&. A copy of the input array is sorted and stored internally\&. If \fIdomain\fP is not specified, returns the scale’s current domain\&.

.PP
# \fIquantile\fP\&.\fBrange\fP([\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIrange\fP is specified, sets the discrete values in the range\&. The array must not be empty, and may contain any type of value\&. The number of values in (the cardinality, or length, of) the \fIrange\fP array determines the number of quantiles that are computed\&. For example, to compute quartiles, \fIrange\fP must be an array of four elements such as [0, 1, 2, 3]\&. If \fIrange\fP is not specified, returns the current range\&.

.PP
# \fIquantile\fP\&.\fBquantiles\fP() · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Returns the quantile thresholds\&. If the \fBrange\fP contains \fIn\fP discrete values, the returned array will contain \fIn\fP - 1 thresholds\&. Values less than the first threshold are considered in the first quantile; values greater than or equal to the first threshold but less than the second threshold are in the second quantile, and so on\&. Internally, the thresholds array is used with \fRbisect\fP to find the output quantile associated with the given input value\&.

.PP
# \fIquantile\fP\&.\fBcopy\fP() · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Returns an exact copy of this scale\&. Changes to this scale will not affect the returned scale, and vice versa\&.
.SS "Threshold Scales"
Threshold scales are similar to \fBquantize scales\fP, except they allow you to map arbitrary subsets of the domain to discrete values in the range\&. The input domain is still continuous, and divided into slices based on a set of threshold values\&. See \fRthis choropleth\fP for an example\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fBscaleThreshold\fP([[\fIdomain\fP, ]\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Constructs a new threshold scale with the specified \fB*domain*\fP and \fB*range*\fP\&. If \fIdomain\fP is not specified, it defaults to [0\&.5]\&. If \fIrange\fP is not specified, it defaults to [0, 1]\&. Thus, the default threshold scale is equivalent to the \fRMath\&.round\fP function for numbers; for example threshold(0\&.49) returns 0, and threshold(0\&.51) returns 1\&.

.PP
# \fIthreshold\fP(\fIvalue\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Given a \fIvalue\fP in the input \fBdomain\fP, returns the corresponding value in the output \fBrange\fP\&. For example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var color = d3\&.scaleThreshold()
    \&.domain([0, 1])
    \&.range(["red", "white", "green"]);

color(\-1);   // "red"
color(0);    // "white"
color(0\&.5);  // "white"
color(1);    // "green"
color(1000); // "green"
.fi
.PP

.PP
# \fIthreshold\fP\&.\fBinvertExtent\fP(\fIvalue\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Returns the extent of values in the \fBdomain\fP [\fIx0\fP, \fIx1\fP] for the corresponding \fIvalue\fP in the \fBrange\fP, representing the inverse mapping from range to domain\&. This method is useful for interaction, say to determine the value in the domain that corresponds to the pixel location under the mouse\&. For example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var color = d3\&.scaleThreshold()
    \&.domain([0, 1])
    \&.range(["red", "white", "green"]);

color\&.invertExtent("red"); // [undefined, 0]
color\&.invertExtent("white"); // [0, 1]
color\&.invertExtent("green"); // [1, undefined]
.fi
.PP

.PP
# \fIthreshold\fP\&.\fBdomain\fP([\fIdomain\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIdomain\fP is specified, sets the scale’s domain to the specified array of values\&. The values must be in ascending order or the behavior of the scale is undefined\&. The values are typically numbers, but any naturally ordered values (such as strings) will work; a threshold scale can be used to encode any type that is ordered\&. If the number of values in the scale’s range is N+1, the number of values in the scale’s domain must be N\&. If there are fewer than N elements in the domain, the additional values in the range are ignored\&. If there are more than N elements in the domain, the scale may return undefined for some inputs\&. If \fIdomain\fP is not specified, returns the scale’s current domain\&.

.PP
# \fIthreshold\fP\&.\fBrange\fP([\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIrange\fP is specified, sets the scale’s range to the specified array of values\&. If the number of values in the scale’s domain is N, the number of values in the scale’s range must be N+1\&. If there are fewer than N+1 elements in the range, the scale may return undefined for some inputs\&. If there are more than N+1 elements in the range, the additional values are ignored\&. The elements in the given array need not be numbers; any value or type will work\&. If \fIrange\fP is not specified, returns the scale’s current range\&.

.PP
# \fIthreshold\fP\&.\fBcopy\fP() · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Returns an exact copy of this scale\&. Changes to this scale will not affect the returned scale, and vice versa\&.
.SS "Ordinal Scales"
Unlike \fBcontinuous scales\fP, ordinal scales have a discrete domain and range\&. For example, an ordinal scale might map a set of named categories to a set of colors, or determine the horizontal positions of columns in a column chart\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fBscaleOrdinal\fP([[\fIdomain\fP, ]\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Constructs a new ordinal scale with the specified \fB*domain*\fP and \fB*range*\fP\&. If \fIdomain\fP is not specified, it defaults to the empty array\&. If \fIrange\fP is not specified, it defaults to the empty array; an ordinal scale always returns undefined until a non-empty range is defined\&.

.PP
# \fIordinal\fP(\fIvalue\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Given a \fIvalue\fP in the input \fBdomain\fP, returns the corresponding value in the output \fBrange\fP\&. If the given \fIvalue\fP is not in the scale’s \fBdomain\fP, returns the \fBunknown\fP; or, if the unknown value is \fBimplicit\fP (the default), then the \fIvalue\fP is implicitly added to the domain and the next-available value in the range is assigned to \fIvalue\fP, such that this and subsequent invocations of the scale given the same input \fIvalue\fP return the same output value\&.

.PP
# \fIordinal\fP\&.\fBdomain\fP([\fIdomain\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIdomain\fP is specified, sets the domain to the specified array of values\&. The first element in \fIdomain\fP will be mapped to the first element in the range, the second domain value to the second range value, and so on\&. Domain values are stored internally in an \fRInternMap\fP from primitive value to index; the resulting index is then used to retrieve a value from the range\&. Thus, an ordinal scale’s values must be coercible to a primitive value, and the primitive domain value uniquely identifies the corresponding range value\&. If \fIdomain\fP is not specified, this method returns the current domain\&.

.PP
Setting the domain on an ordinal scale is optional if the \fBunknown value\fP is \fBimplicit\fP (the default)\&. In this case, the domain will be inferred implicitly from usage by assigning each unique value passed to the scale a new value from the range\&. Note that an explicit domain is recommended to ensure deterministic behavior, as inferring the domain from usage will be dependent on ordering\&.

.PP
# \fIordinal\fP\&.\fBrange\fP([\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIrange\fP is specified, sets the range of the ordinal scale to the specified array of values\&. The first element in the domain will be mapped to the first element in \fIrange\fP, the second domain value to the second range value, and so on\&. If there are fewer elements in the range than in the domain, the scale will reuse values from the start of the range\&. If \fIrange\fP is not specified, this method returns the current range\&.

.PP
# \fIordinal\fP\&.\fBunknown\fP([\fIvalue\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIvalue\fP is specified, sets the output value of the scale for unknown input values and returns this scale\&. If \fIvalue\fP is not specified, returns the current unknown value, which defaults to \fBimplicit\fP\&. The implicit value enables implicit domain construction; see \fB*ordinal*\&.domain\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIordinal\fP\&.\fBcopy\fP() · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Returns an exact copy of this ordinal scale\&. Changes to this scale will not affect the returned scale, and vice versa\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fBscaleImplicit\fP · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
A special value for \fB*ordinal*\&.unknown\fP that enables implicit domain construction: unknown values are implicitly added to the domain\&.
.SS "Band Scales"
Band scales are like \fBordinal scales\fP except the output range is continuous and numeric\&. Discrete output values are automatically computed by the scale by dividing the continuous range into uniform bands\&. Band scales are typically used for bar charts with an ordinal or categorical dimension\&. The \fBunknown value\fP of a band scale is effectively undefined: they do not allow implicit domain construction\&.

.PP

.PP
# d3\&.\fBscaleBand\fP([[\fIdomain\fP, ]\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Constructs a new band scale with the specified \fB*domain*\fP and \fB*range*\fP, no \fBpadding\fP, no \fBrounding\fP and center \fBalignment\fP\&. If \fIdomain\fP is not specified, it defaults to the empty domain\&. If \fIrange\fP is not specified, it defaults to the unit range [0, 1]\&.

.PP
# \fIband\fP(\fIvalue\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Given a \fIvalue\fP in the input \fBdomain\fP, returns the start of the corresponding band derived from the output \fBrange\fP\&. If the given \fIvalue\fP is not in the scale’s domain, returns undefined\&.

.PP
# \fIband\fP\&.\fBdomain\fP([\fIdomain\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIdomain\fP is specified, sets the domain to the specified array of values\&. The first element in \fIdomain\fP will be mapped to the first band, the second domain value to the second band, and so on\&. Domain values are stored internally in an \fRInternMap\fP from primitive value to index; the resulting index is then used to determine the band\&. Thus, a band scale’s values must be coercible to a primitive value, and the primitive domain value uniquely identifies the corresponding band\&. If \fIdomain\fP is not specified, this method returns the current domain\&.

.PP
# \fIband\fP\&.\fBrange\fP([\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIrange\fP is specified, sets the scale’s range to the specified two-element array of numbers\&. If the elements in the given array are not numbers, they will be coerced to numbers\&. If \fIrange\fP is not specified, returns the scale’s current range, which defaults to [0, 1]\&.

.PP
# \fIband\fP\&.\fBrangeRound\fP([\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Sets the scale’s \fB*range*\fP to the specified two-element array of numbers while also enabling \fBrounding\fP\&. This is a convenience method equivalent to:

.PP
.PP
.nf
band
    \&.range(range)
    \&.round(true);
.fi
.PP

.PP
Rounding is sometimes useful for avoiding antialiasing artifacts, though also consider the \fRshape-rendering\fP “crispEdges” styles\&.

.PP
# \fIband\fP\&.\fBround\fP([\fIround\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIround\fP is specified, enables or disables rounding accordingly\&. If rounding is enabled, the start and stop of each band will be integers\&. Rounding is sometimes useful for avoiding antialiasing artifacts, though also consider the \fRshape-rendering\fP “crispEdges” styles\&. Note that if the width of the domain is not a multiple of the cardinality of the range, there may be leftover unused space, even without padding! Use \fB*band*\&.align\fP to specify how the leftover space is distributed\&.

.PP
# \fIband\fP\&.\fBpaddingInner\fP([\fIpadding\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIpadding\fP is specified, sets the inner padding to the specified number which must be less than or equal to 1\&. If \fIpadding\fP is not specified, returns the current inner padding which defaults to 0\&. The inner padding specifies the proportion of the range that is reserved for blank space between bands; a value of 0 means no blank space between bands, and a value of 1 means a \fBbandwidth\fP of zero\&.

.PP
# \fIband\fP\&.\fBpaddingOuter\fP([\fIpadding\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIpadding\fP is specified, sets the outer padding to the specified number which is typically in the range [0, 1]\&. If \fIpadding\fP is not specified, returns the current outer padding which defaults to 0\&. The outer padding specifies the amount of blank space, in terms of multiples of the \fBstep\fP, to reserve before the first band and after the last band\&.

.PP
# \fIband\fP\&.\fBpadding\fP([\fIpadding\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
A convenience method for setting the \fBinner\fP and \fBouter\fP padding to the same \fIpadding\fP value\&. If \fIpadding\fP is not specified, returns the inner padding\&.

.PP
# \fIband\fP\&.\fBalign\fP([\fIalign\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIalign\fP is specified, sets the alignment to the specified value which must be in the range [0, 1]\&. If \fIalign\fP is not specified, returns the current alignment which defaults to 0\&.5\&. The alignment specifies how outer padding is distributed in the range\&. A value of 0\&.5 indicates that the outer padding should be equally distributed before the first band and after the last band; \fIi\&.e\&.\fP, the bands should be centered within the range\&. A value of 0 or 1 may be used to shift the bands to one side, say to position them adjacent to an axis\&. For more, \fRsee this explainer\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIband\fP\&.\fBbandwidth\fP() · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Returns the width of each band\&.

.PP
# \fIband\fP\&.\fBstep\fP() · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Returns the distance between the starts of adjacent bands\&.

.PP
# \fIband\fP\&.\fBcopy\fP() · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Returns an exact copy of this scale\&. Changes to this scale will not affect the returned scale, and vice versa\&.
.SS "Point Scales"
Point scales are a variant of \fBband scales\fP with the bandwidth fixed to zero\&. Point scales are typically used for scatterplots with an ordinal or categorical dimension\&. The \fBunknown value\fP of a point scale is always undefined: they do not allow implicit domain construction\&.

.PP

.PP
# d3\&.\fBscalePoint\fP([[\fIdomain\fP, ]\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Constructs a new point scale with the specified \fB*domain*\fP and \fB*range*\fP, no \fBpadding\fP, no \fBrounding\fP and center \fBalignment\fP\&. If \fIdomain\fP is not specified, it defaults to the empty domain\&. If \fIrange\fP is not specified, it defaults to the unit range [0, 1]\&.

.PP
# \fIpoint\fP(\fIvalue\fP) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Given a \fIvalue\fP in the input \fBdomain\fP, returns the corresponding point derived from the output \fBrange\fP\&. If the given \fIvalue\fP is not in the scale’s domain, returns undefined\&.

.PP
# \fIpoint\fP\&.\fBdomain\fP([\fIdomain\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIdomain\fP is specified, sets the domain to the specified array of values\&. The first element in \fIdomain\fP will be mapped to the first point, the second domain value to the second point, and so on\&. Domain values are stored internally in an \fRInternMap\fP from primitive value to index; the resulting index is then used to determine the point\&. Thus, a point scale’s values must be coercible to a primitive value, and the primitive domain value uniquely identifies the corresponding point\&. If \fIdomain\fP is not specified, this method returns the current domain\&.

.PP
# \fIpoint\fP\&.\fBrange\fP([\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIrange\fP is specified, sets the scale’s range to the specified two-element array of numbers\&. If the elements in the given array are not numbers, they will be coerced to numbers\&. If \fIrange\fP is not specified, returns the scale’s current range, which defaults to [0, 1]\&.

.PP
# \fIpoint\fP\&.\fBrangeRound\fP([\fIrange\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Sets the scale’s \fB*range*\fP to the specified two-element array of numbers while also enabling \fBrounding\fP\&. This is a convenience method equivalent to:

.PP
.PP
.nf
point
    \&.range(range)
    \&.round(true);
.fi
.PP

.PP
Rounding is sometimes useful for avoiding antialiasing artifacts, though also consider the \fRshape-rendering\fP “crispEdges” styles\&.

.PP
# \fIpoint\fP\&.\fBround\fP([\fIround\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIround\fP is specified, enables or disables rounding accordingly\&. If rounding is enabled, the position of each point will be integers\&. Rounding is sometimes useful for avoiding antialiasing artifacts, though also consider the \fRshape-rendering\fP “crispEdges” styles\&. Note that if the width of the domain is not a multiple of the cardinality of the range, there may be leftover unused space, even without padding! Use \fB*point*\&.align\fP to specify how the leftover space is distributed\&.

.PP
# \fIpoint\fP\&.\fBpadding\fP([\fIpadding\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIpadding\fP is specified, sets the outer padding to the specified number which is typically in the range [0, 1]\&. If \fIpadding\fP is not specified, returns the current outer padding which defaults to 0\&. The outer padding specifies the amount of blank space, in terms of multiples of the \fBstep\fP, to reserve before the first point and after the last point\&. Equivalent to \fB*band*\&.paddingOuter\fP\&.

.PP
# \fIpoint\fP\&.\fBalign\fP([\fIalign\fP]) · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
If \fIalign\fP is specified, sets the alignment to the specified value which must be in the range [0, 1]\&. If \fIalign\fP is not specified, returns the current alignment which defaults to 0\&.5\&. The alignment specifies how any leftover unused space in the range is distributed\&. A value of 0\&.5 indicates that the leftover space should be equally distributed before the first point and after the last point; \fIi\&.e\&.\fP, the points should be centered within the range\&. A value of 0 or 1 may be used to shift the points to one side, say to position them adjacent to an axis\&.

.PP
# \fIpoint\fP\&.\fBbandwidth\fP() · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Returns zero\&.

.PP
# \fIpoint\fP\&.\fBstep\fP() · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Returns the distance between the starts of adjacent points\&.

.PP
# \fIpoint\fP\&.\fBcopy\fP() · \fRSource\fP, \fRExamples\fP

.PP
Returns an exact copy of this scale\&. Changes to this scale will not affect the returned scale, and vice versa\&. 
